Is it possible to arrange 12 identical coins along the walls of a large square box such that there are exactly:
a) 2 coins along each wall;
b) 3 coins along each wall;
c) 4 coins along each wall;
d) 5 coins along each wall;
e) 6 coins along each wall;
f) 7 coins along each wall?

(Stacking coins one on top of the other is allowed.) For those cases where it is possible, draw how to do it. For the other cases, prove that such an arrangement is impossible.

Find all polynomials with two variables that satisfy the following conditions:
1. \( P \) is homogeneous of degree \( n \), i.e., for all real numbers \( t, x, y \), \( P(t x, t y) = t^{n} \cdot P(x, y) \);
2. For all real numbers \( a, b, c \), \( P(a+b, c) + P(b+c, a) + P(c+a, b) = 0 \);
3. \( P(1,0)=1 \).

Given \( a_1 = 1 \), \( a_2 = 2 \), and the recurrence relation:
\[
a_{n+2} = 
\begin{cases} 
5a_{n+1} - 3a_{n}, & \text{if } a_n \cdot a_{n+1} \text{ is even} \\
a_{n+1} - a_{n}, & \text{if } a_n \cdot a_{n+1} \text{ is odd}
\end{cases}
\]
prove that for all \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), \( a_n \neq 0 \).

Prove the identity

$$
\begin{aligned}
& (a x+b y+c z+d u)^{2}+(b x+c y+d z+a u)^{2}+(c x+d y+a z+b u)^{2}+ \\
& +(d x+a y+b z+c u)^{2}= \\
& =(d x+c y+b z+a u)^{2}+(c x+b y+a z+d u)^{2}+(b x+a y+d z+c u)^{2}+ \\
& +(a x+d y+c z+b u)^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

s:
We need to prove the inequality:
$$
\frac{1-2x}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}} + \frac{1-2y}{\sqrt{y(1-y)}} + \frac{1-2z}{\sqrt{z(1-z)}} \geq \sqrt{\frac{x}{1-x}} + \sqrt{\frac{y}{1-y}} + \sqrt{\frac{z}{1-z}}
$$
under the constraint \(x + y + z = 1\).

1. Start with rewriting the original inequality by isolating the left-hand side:
\begin{align*}
\sum \left(\frac{1-2x}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}} + 2\sqrt{\frac{x}{1-x}} \right) &\geq 3 \sum \sqrt{\frac{x}{1-x}}
\end{align*}
This simplifies to:
\begin{align*}
\sum \frac{1}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}} &\geq 3 \sum \sqrt{\frac{x}{1-x}}
\end{align*}

2. Notice that the sequences \(x, y, z\) and \(\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{y(1-y)}}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{z(1-z)}}\right)\) exhibit a specific ordering property:
    - If \(x \leq y\), then \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}} \geq \frac{1}{\sqrt{y(1-y)}}\).
    - This equivalently transforms to \(\sqrt{x(1-x)} \leq \sqrt{y(1-y)}\)

3. To verify this:
    - Consider \(x \leq y\), then:
    \begin{align*}
    \sqrt{x(1-x)} &\leq \sqrt{y(1-y)} \\
    x(1-x) &\leq y(1-y) \\
    x - x^2 &\leq y - y^2 \\
    (x - y)(1 - (x + y)) &\leq 0,
    \end{align*}
    which holds for \(x + y + z = 1\).

4. We can now apply the rearrangement inequality. Rearrangement inequality states that for two similarly sorted sequences \((a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n)\) and \((b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n)\), the sum of the products of these sequences is maximized when both sequences are similarly sorted:
\begin{align*}
\sum \frac{1}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}} &\geq 3\sum \sqrt{\frac{x}{1-x}}
\end{align*}

### Conclusion

This completing the analysis, we have proved the given inequality:
$$
\frac{1-2x}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}} + \frac{1-2y}{\sqrt{y(1-y)}} + \frac{1-2z}{\sqrt{z(1-z)}} \geq \sqrt{\frac{x}{1-x}} + \sqrt{\frac{y}{1-y}} + \sqrt{\frac{z}{1-z}}
$$
under the constraint \(x + y + z = 1\).

\(\blacksquare\)

